Subject: Read forwarding keeps a per-block last-writer index

What this does
  Read forwarding keeps a per-block last-writer index.

Why we need it
  The forward-a-frame-read pass scanned every instruction between a store and a read to test for a clobber, quadratic on large functions.

What it adds
  Buckets instructions per block (`bstart_insn`) and keeps `lreg`, the index of the last instruction writing each physical register, for an O clobber test; `lreg` is sized by the register count (not the value count) and the buffers use `js_malloc_rt`, so the optional pass skips on allocation failure - including the zero-value case that previously left a pending out-of-memory exception on module bodies - instead of throwing.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
